            title: "Resource Acquisition Is Initialization",
            concept: "RAII means acquiring a resource in a constructor and releasing it in the matching destructor, so cleanup is automatic. It ties the lifetime of a resource to the lifetime of an object, so cleanup happens automatically when the object goes out of scope. This makes code shorter and much harder to leak resources from.",
            syntax: "class Guard { public: Guard() { acquire(); } ~Guard() { release(); } };",
            exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n\nclass Guard {\n public:\n  explicit Guard(const std::string& name) : name_(name) {\n    std::cout << \"acquired \" << name_ << \"\\n\";\n  }\n  ~Guard() { std::cout << \"released \" << name_ << \"\\n\"; }\n  const std::string& name() const { return name_; }\n private:\n  std::string name_;\n};\n\nint main() {\n  Guard item(\"guard-1\");\n  std::cout << \"using \" << item.name() << \"\\n\";\n}\n",
